WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.860
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.160
In this piece of work, we are interested in
simulating the dynamics of water waves.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.720
There are quite a few forces acting on a bucket
of water, such as surface tension, internal

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:20.820
pressure, external force fields (such as wind,
for instance), and gravity.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.659
Therefore, it is not a surprise that these
waves can become quite complex with a lot

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:29.609
of high-frequency details that are difficult
to simulate.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.190
Accurately modeling wave reflections after
colliding with solids is also an important

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.629
and highly sought-after detail to capture.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:41.489
This piece of work simulates Sir George Biddell
Airy's dispersion model.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:44.010
Now what does this mean exactly?

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:49.559
The Airy model describes many common wave
phenomena accurately, such as how longer waves

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:54.989
are dominated by gravitational forces and
how shorter waves dance mostly according the

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:56.850
will of surface tension.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.989
However, as amazing this theory is, it does
not formulate these quantities in a way that

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:05.110
would be directly applicable to a computer
simulation.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.790
The main contribution of this paper is a new
convolution formulation of this model and

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:15.010
some more optimizations that can be directly
added into a simulation, and not only that,

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:21.140
but the resulting algorithm parallelizes and
maps well to the graphical card in our computers.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.910
We have earlier discussed what a convolution
is.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:28.980
Essentially, it is a mathematical operation
that can add reverberation to the sound of

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.190
our guitar, or accurately simulate how light
bounces around under our skin.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.600
Links to these episodes are available in the
video description and at the end of the video,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.740
check them out, I am sure you'll have a lot
of fun with them!

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.820
Regarding applications - as the technique
obeys Airy's classical dispersion model, I

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.800
expect and hope this to be useful for ocean
and coastal engineering and in simulating

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.550
huge tidal waves.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:58.440
Note that limitations apply, for instance,
the original linear theory is mostly good

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.800
for shallow water simulations and larger waves
in deeper waters.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.880
The proposed approximation itself also has
inherent limitations, such as the possibility

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.770
of waves going through thinner objects.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:16.100
The resulting algorithm is, however, very
accurate and honestly, a joy to watch.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:22.200
It is also shown to support larger-scale scenes
- here you see how beautifully it can simulate

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.820
the capillary waves produced by these rain
drops and of course, the waves around the

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.390
swans in the pond.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.310
This example took roughly one and a half second
per frame to compute.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.410
You know the drill - a couple more followup
papers down the line, and it will surely run

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:37.700
in real time.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.860
Can't wait!

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.860
Also, please let me know in the comments section
whether you have found this episode understandable.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.150
Was it easy to follow?

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.290
Too much?

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.410
Your feedback is, as always, highly appreciated.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:53.050
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

